<description>
Paint a red square around each yellow marker, sized to stop just before the nearest grey marker.

Input structure
- A black background contains one or more yellow marker pixels and one or more grey blocker pixels.
- The output has the same shape as the input and preserves the yellow and grey pixels.

Rule
- For each yellow marker, find the minimum Chebyshev distance to any grey blocker.
- Fill the centered square of radius one less than that distance with red, changing only background pixels.

Key insight
- Grey pixels define the first forbidden Chebyshev ring around each yellow marker, so the red square is the
  largest centered square that remains one cell short of every grey blocker.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "yellow marker pixels and grey blocker pixels on a black background",
  "transform": "around each yellow marker, fill background pixels in the centered red square whose radius is one less than the nearest grey pixel's Chebyshev distance; preserve yellow and grey pixels"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 9-30 rows x 8-20 cols, black background
  markers:
    yellow: 1-3 pixels, these are the square centers
    grey: 1-10 pixels, these are blockers that limit square size
  colors_present: exactly yellow and grey on black background (input); adds red (output)
  transform:
    - for each yellow pixel, find min Chebyshev distance D to any grey pixel
    - fill a (2*(D-1)+1)-square centered on yellow with red, only replacing black pixels
    - radius = D-1, range 1-5; the red square is the largest that stops one cell short of every grey blocker
  constraints:
    - radius >= 1 for every yellow marker (so min Chebyshev dist to any grey >= 2)
    - red square must fit entirely within grid (no clipping)
    - red squares of different yellow markers must not overlap [observed]
    - yellow and grey pixels are preserved in output (red only replaces black)

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. choose grid size H x W (9-30 x 8-20) and number of yellow markers N (1-3)
    2. for each yellow marker, pick a radius R (1-5); place yellow at position (r,c) such that the (2R+1)-square fits in grid (r >= R, r+R < H, c >= R, c+R < W)
    3. ensure squares do not overlap (Chebyshev between any two yellows > sum of their radii)
    4. for each yellow marker, place at least one grey pixel at Chebyshev distance exactly R+1 (this is the constraining blocker); ensure grey is within grid bounds
    5. optionally scatter additional grey pixels, each at Chebyshev distance > R+1 from its nearest yellow (so they don't reduce any radius below R)
    6. verify no grey pixel is within Chebyshev distance R of any yellow (would invalidate radius)
    7. input = black grid with yellow + grey pixels; output = run solver
```
</generation_spec>
</reference_puzzle>

